<h2>How the calculation works</h2><p>Each stage conversion rate divides the later-stage count by the immediately preceding stage. Overall applicant-to-hire conversion divides hires by applicants.</p><h2>How to use the result</h2><p>Use the funnel to locate where candidate volume narrows most. A low stage conversion may reflect sourcing quality, screening criteria, interview capacity, compensation, candidate experience or market conditions depending on the stage.</p><h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2><p>Period-based funnels can mix cohorts: candidates who applied before the period may be interviewed during it, causing a later-stage count to exceed an earlier stage. Cohort-based reporting gives cleaner conversion analysis when available.</p><h2>Example</h2><p>With 500 applicants and 15 hires, overall applicant-to-hire conversion is 3%, but stage rates show where most of the narrowing occurred.</p>
<?php $toolContent=ob_get_clean();$faqs=[['Why can a later stage count be higher than an earlier one?','Period reports can mix candidate cohorts. People entering a later stage this month may have applied in a previous month.'],['Should I use unique candidates or activities?','Use consistent unique-candidate counts for a true funnel unless your reporting definition explicitly uses activities.'],['What does a low screen-to-interview rate mean?','It shows a narrow transition, but the reason requires context such as qualification quality, role requirements or interview capacity.'],['Can I compare roles?','Yes, but compare similar role types and periods because funnel behavior can vary substantially by market and seniority.']];require __DIR__.'/../includes/tool-template.php';
